A skoro model is a directed acyclic graph that you never touch directly. You hold a
**client**, ask it to add nodes, and get back typed **handles** that behave like values.

## The client

`ModelClient<B>` is a cheap, cloneable handle to a `ModelServer<B>`, which owns the graph. Most
code is written against two traits rather than the concrete client:

| Trait | Can | Typical holder |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `ModelReaderOps` | evaluate, differentiate and generate; read terms and bindings; add constants | an estimator or sampler |
| `ModelBuilderOps` | everything above, plus anything that changes the model: add density terms (`add_quantity`, `add_proposal`, `add_factor`) and parameters, `observe`, `demote_to_parameter`, `stacked_tangent`, `remove` and `remove_closure` | code that builds or conditions models |

> [!DECISION] Two tiers
> An estimator can be handed a model it cannot change: it cannot add or remove terms, observe, or
> demote. See
> [Client tiers](../../design/client-tiers/).

## Quantities

Every node you add comes back as a `Q<B, M>`, where `M` is the quantity's type: a manifold such as
`Real`, `Euclidean<D>`/`EuclideanD`, `Tril`, `SO3` or `SE3`, or a discrete type such as
`Categorical`. There are three ways to add one:

- `add_constant(M, &value)`: a fixed value, known at build time.
- `add_parameter(M)`: a free value, supplied at evaluation time and differentiated with respect to.
- `add_quantity(distribution)`: a stochastic quantity with a conditional distribution. This also
  adds a term to the log-density. See [Distributions and factors](../distributions-and-factors/).

A stochastic quantity can later be pinned to data with `observe(&q, &value)`.

## Operations

A handle combines with other handles through `std::ops` operators and `*Ops` extension traits, and
each operation adds a *mapped* node:

```rust
{../../../../crates/skoro/src/lib.rs:34..37}
```

<ModelGraph spec={aPlusB} />

The operations that exist depend on the quantity's type: matrix products for matrices,
`compose`/`rotate`/`log` for rotations, `log`/`abs` for reals and vectors, and so on. See
[Operations on quantities](../../concepts/quantity-operations/) for the list.

## Ownership and cleanup

A `Q` is an **ownership token**. When every handle that reaches a node has been dropped, and no
likelihood term reaches it, the node is garbage and a later `cleanup()` reclaims it. A bare `Id`
(from `q.id()`) is a *weak* reference, and it may name nothing after a cleanup. That includes a
parameter: one that no handle names and no term reads is collected too, so keep a `Q` for anything
you mean to optimize.

> [!DECISION]
> Garbage collection is mark-and-sweep from live handles and terms. See
> [Handles own nodes](../../design/ownership/).

